  "question": {
    "text": "A cavity of the radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> is made inside a solid sphere of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. The centre of the cavity is located at a distance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> from the centre of the sphere. The gravitational force on a particle of mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> at a distance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> from the centre of the sphere on the line joining both the centres of sphere and cavity is (opposite to the centre of the cavity).<br />\r[Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> is the mass of the sphere]"
